WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our next guest is a very
talented actress and writer

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.838
you know from shows
such as "Rutherford Falls,"

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.539
"American Gods,"
and "Reservation Dogs."

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:07.707
She stars in "Echo,"

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:09.742
which is streaming now
on Disney+ and Hulu.

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:10.810
Let's take a look.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:35.435
Please welcome to the show
Devery Jacobs, everybody.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:37.437
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:56.122 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.024
Welcome to the show.
How are you?

00:00:58.024 --> 00:00:59.692
-I'm great.
I'm in New York. Hi!

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:01.027
-Very nice.

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:03.529
And now you lived here
10 years ago very briefly,

00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:04.797
but a different New York

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:06.766
than you're probably
experiencing on this trip.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.768
-Oh, 100%.
I lived here as, like,

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:12.671
a poor, aspiring actor
10 years ago,

00:01:12.671 --> 00:01:16.642
and I lived in, like, a bedroom,
one -- like, a tiny bedroom

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:19.745
that had no windows and, like,
was infested with cockroaches.

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:23.649
So now to be here and to have
Marvel be putting me up,

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:25.585
it's like a little bit
of a different experience

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:26.886
than that was.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:29.722
-Always try to
get Marvel to pay for it.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:31.657
-Yeah, I'm learning.
-Yeah, you're learning.

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:34.427
Before we move on to "Echo,"
which is terrific,

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.629
I want to congratulate you
and, basically,

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.497
everybody involved with
"Reservation Dogs."

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:40.999
Was such a special show.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:40.999 --> 00:01:43.036
Three incredible seasons.

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:46.205
And you had such a cool arc

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:48.441
as an actor on that show,

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:51.878
because you also joined the
writers room and got to direct.

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:53.946
-That's right.
For the first season,

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:56.582
I was an actor,
and like Sterlin Harjo,

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:58.617
who's the showrunner
and the creator of the show,

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:00.953
like, he's noted me for years,
not only as an actor

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:03.623
but also, like, as a filmmaker,
writer-director.

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.525
So for season one, I was acting,
and I was just like,

00:02:06.525 --> 00:02:08.527
"Hey, would I be able
to shadow a director?"

00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:10.830
And Sterlin was like,
"Yeah, of course."

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.798
I mean, FX, like, bless them,
they're great.

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.934
They we e like, "Yeah, we kind
of brought you here for acting,

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:18.337
so if you could, like, focus
on that, that would be great."

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:19.606
But then, for season two,

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.607
I came in with, like,
my references,

00:02:21.607 --> 00:02:23.209
I was, "Here's
all my writing samples.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.611
Like, I'd love to be
in the writers room."

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:27.347
And Sterlin was basically like,

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:28.581
"Yeah,
you don't have to do all that.

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:31.150
Like, come in.
Like, be in the writers room."

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.754
And so, from there on,
I was able to co-write

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:38.291
an episode in season two,
write a solo episode,

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:42.261
episode nine of season three,
where Elora Danan meets her dad,

00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:44.530
which turns out to be
Ethan Hawke -- spoiler alert.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.898
[ Laughter ]

00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:47.767
And, um --
And then also got to direct

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.902
an episode in season three.

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:52.305
-It is a show where I imagine
being in the writers room

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:54.774
is so special
because it was, you know,

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:57.009
critically lauded
for its authenticity.

00:02:57.009 --> 00:02:58.844
And being one of those
really authentic voices

00:02:58.844 --> 00:03:00.246
must have been a cool feeling,

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:02.014
knowing that you were making
the show what it was.

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:04.217
-Oh, it was incredible.
Like, everyone

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:07.186
in the entire show,
whether it was directors,

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.188
whether it was actors
or writers,

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:12.458
everyone was from an Indigenous
community and different nations.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:14.426
So it was something
that was really special.

00:03:14.426 --> 00:03:16.629
It was a show
that was really groundbreaking.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:20.499
It's bittersweet that we're on
our final season releasing,

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:22.535
and it's going to leave
a big hole in the industry,

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.603
but it's the hope
that there'll be many,

00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:26.171
many more to come from there.

00:03:26.171 --> 00:03:29.442
-This is also, when we speak
of authenticity, in "Echo,"

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:33.212
which is wonderful.
You have to, obviously,

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:35.214
we saw, do a lot of
American Sign Language.

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:38.318
Was that something
that you have to teach yourself?

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:40.152
-I didn't teach myself, no.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:42.388
There were really
incredible teachers

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.756
from the Sign Language Center.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:46.292
There was also Douglas Ridloff,
who is the ASL master

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:47.626
and one of the producers
on the project,

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:50.630
who was just, like, fantastic.
But, yeah,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.898
getting to learn
that language was a privilege,

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:55.601
but also, like, a lot of
pressure and making sure

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.970
that we were able to,
like, communicate with Alaqua,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:02.441
who's, like, a legend in her
own right and is so badass.

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:05.378
It was -- it was really awesome
to be able to do that.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:07.146
And it's something
that I'm still learning

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:10.149
even after --
after the show's wrapped.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.451
-So, you were -- you played
a character, Bonnie, in this

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:13.919
that was not in the comics,
which is very --

00:04:13.919 --> 00:04:15.454
-Yes.
-When they make a character

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:17.189
for you in a Marvel show,
that's a big deal.

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:18.924
-Oh, 100%. It was great.

00:04:18.924 --> 00:04:20.659
-But you -- also, not your first
Marvel role.

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:24.563
You were in "What If...?"
and you voiced this character,

00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:26.666
which is
also pretty badass.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:27.900
-Thanks.
-And was that --

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.601
This was your first entry
into Marvel.

00:04:29.601 --> 00:04:31.337
And was this a cool experience,
getting to do this show?

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:34.039
-It was incredible.
So, I auditioned for

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:36.275
"What If...?"
and the character of Kahhori,

00:04:36.275 --> 00:04:38.111
who is Mohawk,
and I'm also Mohawk.

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:40.380
I grew up in Kahnawake
Mohawk territory,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.282
and they were looking
for Mohawk speakers.

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:45.384
I am not
a fluent Mohawk speaker,

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.353
so this was a lot of pressure

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:49.088
also to make sure
that we got it right.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:51.824
My doda, my grandmother,
is the principal --

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:53.392
used to be the principal
of the Mohawk Immersion

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:54.927
Elementary School.

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.495
My niece is a first-language
Mohawk speaker,

00:04:56.495 --> 00:05:00.566
and so it was really special to
be able to work on Kanien'kéha

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:02.602
and to be able to bring
that character to life.

00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:05.938
But Marvel actually didn't --
Like, the "Echo" people

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:07.473
didn't know
that I was already

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.675
in another Marvel project,

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:12.044
and they asked on set,
and I ended up bringing it up,

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:13.546
and they were like,
"Oh, we had no idea."

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.281
And I'm like, "I know,
because I didn't tell you that."

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:16.548
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.548 --> 00:05:18.151
But I couldn't have imagined
that, like,

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.319
these two very different
Marvel projects

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:22.521
would have come out, like,
within weeks of each other.

00:05:22.521 --> 00:05:25.023
-It's very nice.
It speaks to your range.

00:05:25.023 --> 00:05:26.225
Congratulations.
-Oh, thanks!

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:27.292
[ Laughter ]

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:29.995
You did not, uh --
[ Applause ]

00:05:29.995 --> 00:05:32.565
Acting was not
your first choice for a career.

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:34.167
Or, I should say, you probably
always wanted to be an actor,

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:35.768
but you had different plans
for yourself, professionally.

00:05:35.768 --> 00:05:38.871
-I mean, I always wanted to be
in film and television.

00:05:38.871 --> 00:05:41.274
It was my dream.
But, like, growing up on my rez,

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:44.543
there was really no clear path
to do that.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:46.979
So I actually, like,
could be considered

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:49.248
a certified corrections officer.
-Okay.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.584
-Um, which was
a very different path,

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.519
but I wanted to be able to go
towards social work,

00:05:54.519 --> 00:05:56.822
which would have had, like,
the applicable credits.

00:05:56.822 --> 00:05:59.492
And I figured
if I wasn't able to pursue

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:01.159
my love of film and TV,

00:06:01.159 --> 00:06:03.629
then I wanted to be able
to help Indigenous people.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:04.797
Um, and then I was
cast in a role,

00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:06.031
and I was like,
"I can't do anything else.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:07.499
I just --
I love this too much."

00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:09.668
But the hope is that I'm able
to do both of those things

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:12.605
of, like, advocate
for Indigenous rights

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:16.208
and also be able to
be a storyteller in front

00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:17.743
and behind the camera
in this medium.

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.245
-The fact that you can act,
write, direct,

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:21.246
and play two Marvel characters
in the same year,

00:06:21.246 --> 00:06:22.614
I feel like there's no reason
to think

00:06:22.614 --> 00:06:24.549
you won't be able to do
everything you set your mind to.

00:06:24.549 --> 00:06:25.785
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:27.353
What a delight to meet you.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:29.321
-You guys, that's Devery Jacobs.

00:06:29.321 --> 00:06:31.290
"Echo" is streaming now
on Disney+ and Hulu.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:33.059
We'll be right back
with Nico Carney.

